  Dwi = `This chat isn't linked to a computer right now, so the browsers on the user's computer can't be used here, and no tool here can link one, including the tools whose names start with enable__. That is a normal state, not an outage. You have the cloud browser: its tools are the ones whose names start with mcp__${wp}__ (load any that was only announced through ${as} before you call it). Unless the user asked for a browser on their own computer, by any of its names or by describing it, do this step in the cloud browser and carry on without waiting for the user: don't bring up that the chat isn't linked, and don't suggest linking a computer or opening the desktop app. Your having called this tool is not such a request. If they did ask for their own browser, don't use the cloud browser yet: tell them in one or two plain sentences, in their language and without tool names, that you can't use that browser because this chat isn't linked to a computer right now, and that opening this chat in the Claude desktop app on a computer and sending a message can link it, then ask whether to use the cloud browser instead. A card about using one of their computers may appear under your reply: if their next message is only "Continue" or the name of a computer and you now have tools that run on their computer (their names start with mcp__${zo}__ or mcp__${Ta}__), go on with what they asked for, with those tools and not the cloud browser.`,
  Lwi = `Claude in Chrome is switched off for this chat, and no tool here can switch it on. Tell the user so in one or two plain sentences, in their language and without tool names. For example: "I can't use Chrome from here, because Claude in Chrome is switched off for this chat. You can switch it on under Connectors in this chat, on the web or in the Claude desktop app, if your organization allows Claude in Chrome." Then, if you have tools whose names contain Claude_Browser, offer to use the browser built into the Claude desktop app instead; otherwise do the task another way with the tools you have here if you can. Say nothing about what you could do once it is on, don't say that Chrome or its extension isn't connected, and suggest no other steps or settings for Claude in Chrome, with one exception: if the user says the switch already shows on, ask them to switch it off and on again there. If the user says they can't switch it on there, say only that you can't tell why from here, and that an organization can block Claude in Chrome for its members: no other cause, and no step. If the user says they switched it on, use Claude in Chrome's tools if you now have any (their names contain claude-in-chrome or Claude_in_Chrome); otherwise say only that you still can't use it here: no cause, and no step such as refreshing or waiting.`,
  Nwi = `Claude in Chrome is switched off for this chat, and this chat isn't linked to a computer right now. No tool here can change either, including ${Pto} and the tools whose names start with enable__. Both are normal states, not an outage or a connection problem. While you have none of Claude in Chrome's tools (their names contain ${Ta}__ or Claude_in_Chrome__) there is no point looking or waiting for them. Either state can change during the conversation: if such tools are later in your tool list or announced to you, use them, whatever this result says. Tell the user both in three or four plain sentences, in their language and without tool names, then do the task another way with the tools you have here if you can. For example: "I can't use Chrome from here: Claude in Chrome is switched off for this chat, and the chat isn't linked to a computer right now. You can switch Claude in Chrome on under Connectors in this chat, on the web or in the Claude desktop app, if your organization allows Claude in Chrome. If you have the desktop app on a computer, opening this chat there and sending a message can link it. If I still can't use your computer after that, adding a folder from that computer to this chat, in the desktop app, can." Say nothing about what you could do after that or when that might be, and suggest no other steps or settings for either, with one exception: if the user says the switch already shows on, ask them to switch it off and on again there. If the user says they can't switch it on there, say only that you can't tell why from here, and that an organization can block Claude in Chrome for its members: no other cause, and no step. If the user says they did any of these and you still have none of Claude in Chrome's tools, say that you still can't use it here, with no cause, and name again only the steps they have not said they tried. If they say they tried all of them and you still have none, say only that you still can't use it here: no cause, and no step such as refreshing or waiting.`,
  WRn = "This call enables nothing, and no tool here can add Claude in Chrome: this chat has none of its tools right now. Tell the user in one plain sentence, in their language and without tool names, that you can't use Claude in Chrome in this chat right now. Then, if you have tools whose names contain Claude_Browser, offer to use the browser built into the Claude desktop app instead; otherwise do the task another way with the tools you have here if you can. You don't know the cause: don't say that Chrome or its extension isn't connected, and suggest no steps or settings for Claude in Chrome. If you later have its tools (their names contain claude-in-chrome or Claude_in_Chrome), use them.";
